var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#Reference","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Shapes","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Zones-and-layouts","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Zones and layouts"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#The-window","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"The window"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Catalogs","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Catalogs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#On-a-grid","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"On a grid"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#For-a-mesher","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"For a mesher"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Index","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Index"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#EasyDraw.EasyDraw","page":"Reference","text":"EasyDraw\n\nDraw the zones of a 2D (x–z) model in a window, or write them in code, and get them back as data for a solver: the zone or a property at any coordinates, for finite differences and particles, or disjoint regions written as a Gmsh script, for finite elements.\n\nShapes and zones: Polygon, Block, Ball, Region, Zone; a point belongs to the last zone that contains it.\nThe model: Layout, save_layout, load_layout.\nThe window: draw, offering what its host's Catalog lists.\nOn a grid: zone_at, zone_index, property, points.\nFor a mesher: partition, write_geo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.EasyDraw"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EasyDraw.AbstractShape","page":"Reference","text":"AbstractShape\n\nThe supertype of a zone's shape: Polygon, Block, Ball or Region. inside(shape, x) tells whether a point lies in it.\n\nA new shape is a subtype with an inside method. Like a Region, it then works in code (sampling, points) but cannot be written to a file, drawn in the window or partitioned.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.AbstractShape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EasyDraw.Polygon","page":"Reference","text":"Polygon\n\nA polygon in the x–z plane, of one outline or several. In 3D it is extruded along y.\n\nA point is inside when it lies inside an odd number of the outlines (the even–odd rule), so several outlines make one zone of several parts, or with holes. The window leaves a zone in this form when a later zone cuts it.\n\nFields\n\nvertices::Vector{Vector{Float64}}: the [x, z] vertices of every outline, one outline after the other, the first vertex of each not repeated at its end.\nsizes::Vector{Int}: how many vertices each outline has.\n\nConstructors\n\nPolygon(vertices)          # one outline: a vector of [x, z] vertices\nPolygon(rings)             # several outlines: a vector of such vectors\nPolygon(vertices, sizes)   # the fields themselves\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: when an outline has fewer than 3 vertices, a vertex is not two coordinates, or sizes does not add up to the number of vertices.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> square = Polygon([[0, 0], [4, 0], [4, 4], [0, 4]]);\n\njulia> inside(square, (1.0, 1.0))\ntrue\n\njulia> frame = Polygon([[[0, 0], [4, 0], [4, 4], [0, 4]], [[1, 1], [3, 1], [3, 3], [1, 3]]]);   # with a hole\n\njulia> inside(frame, (2.0, 2.0)), frame.sizes\n(false, [4, 4])\n\nSee also polygon_rings, inside.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.Polygon"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EasyDraw.Block","page":"Reference","text":"Block\n\nThe axis-aligned box lo ≤ x ≤ hi, its bounds included. Given by two coordinates (x, z), it is extruded along y in 3D.\n\nFields\n\nlo::Vector{Float64}: the lower corner.\nhi::Vector{Float64}: the upper corner.\n\nConstructors\n\nBlock(lo, hi)\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: when lo and hi differ in length, or lo exceeds hi along an axis.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> b = Block([0, 0], [10, 2]);\n\njulia> inside(b, (10.0, 2.0)), inside(b, (5.0, 3.0))\n(true, false)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.Block"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EasyDraw.Ball","page":"Reference","text":"Ball\n\nThe points at most radius from centre: a disk in 2D, a sphere in 3D, or, with a two-coordinate (x, z) centre in 3D, a cylinder along y. The circle itself is included.\n\nFields\n\ncentre::Vector{Float64}: the centre.\nradius::Float64: the radius.\n\nConstructors\n\nBall(centre, radius)\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: when radius is negative.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> lens = Ball([5, 2], 1.0);\n\njulia> inside(lens, (5.5, 2.5)), inside(lens, (6.5, 2.0))\n(true, false)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.Ball"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EasyDraw.Region","page":"Reference","text":"Region{F}\n\nThe points x for which f(x) is true: any shape, written as a Julia function. x holds the point's coordinates, (x, z) in 2D, so x[end] is its height in 2D and 3D alike.\n\nA Region works in code only: it cannot be written to a file, drawn in the window or partitioned.\n\nFields\n\nf::F: the predicate, returning a Bool.\n\nConstructors\n\nRegion(f)\n\nExamples\n\njulia> above = Region(x -> x[end] > 0.5x[1]);   # above the line z = x/2\n\njulia> inside(above, (2.0, 3.0))\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.Region"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.inside","page":"Reference","text":"inside(shape::AbstractShape, x) -> Bool\n\nWhether the point x (its coordinates, (x, z) or (x, y, z)) lies in shape:\n\na Polygon by the even–odd rule over all its outlines;\na Block with its bounds included;\na Ball as its closed disk or sphere;\na Region by its predicate.\n\nA shape given by two coordinates reads a 3D point's x and z, so it is extruded along y.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: when a Block or Ball has neither as many coordinates as the point nor two.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> inside(Ball([0, 0], 1.0), (0.5, 0.5))\ntrue\n\njulia> inside(Block([0, 0], [1, 1]), (0.5, 7.0, 0.5))   # an (x, z) block in 3D: extruded along y\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.inside"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.inpolygon","page":"Reference","text":"inpolygon(x, z, vertices) -> Bool\n\nWhether the point (x, z) lies inside the polygon with these vertices ([x, z] pairs, the first not repeated at the end), by the even–odd rule, so that concave and self-touching polygons work. A point exactly on an edge may count as either side.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> inpolygon(1.0, 1.0, [[0, 0], [4, 0], [4, 4], [0, 4]])\ntrue\n\nSee also inside, Polygon.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.inpolygon"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.polygon_rings","page":"Reference","text":"polygon_rings(polygon::Polygon) -> Vector{Vector{Vector{Float64}}}\n\nThe outlines of polygon, each a vector of its [x, z] vertices.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> polygon_rings(Polygon([[[0, 0], [4, 0], [4, 4]], [[1, 1], [2, 1], [2, 2]]]))\n2-element Vector{Vector{Vector{Float64}}}:\n [[0.0, 0.0], [4.0, 0.0], [4.0, 4.0]]\n [[1.0, 1.0], [2.0, 1.0], [2.0, 2.0]]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.polygon_rings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EasyDraw.Zone","page":"Reference","text":"Zone\n\nA named part of a model: the points in its shape, with properties of any name. Where zones overlap, a point belongs to the last zone that contains it.\n\nFields\n\nname::String: the zone's name.\nshape::AbstractShape: its Polygon, Block, Ball or Region.\nprops::Dict{Symbol,Any}: its properties. A number is stored as a Float64, a list of numbers as a Vector{Float64}, anything else as given.\n\nConstructors\n\nZone(name, shape; props...)\nZone(name, shape, props::AbstractDict)\n\nExamples\n\njulia> z = Zone(\"lens\", Ball([5, 2], 1.0); rho=2100, note=\"wet\");\n\njulia> z.props[:rho], z.props[:note]\n(2100.0, \"wet\")\n\nSee also Layout, zone_at.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.Zone"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EasyDraw.Layout","page":"Reference","text":"Layout\n\nA 2D (x–z) model, as drawn in the window or written in code: its domain, its zones, a boundary kind per side, and the values its host's Catalog asks for. Values are kept as given: SI in, SI out.\n\nFields\n\nL::Vector{Float64}: the domain's size [Lx, Lz]: the domain is [0, Lx] × [0, Lz].\nnel::Vector{Int}: the number of cells along x and z.\nni::Int: points per cell and axis, where the window previews points and points samples (1: the cell centres).\nwidth::Union{Nothing,Float64}: a width along y, to extrude the model in 3D; nothing in 2D.\nnely::Union{Nothing,Int}: the number of cells along y, with width.\nzones::Vector{Zone}: the zones, in order: where they overlap, the later one wins.\nsides::Dict{String,String}: a boundary kind per side, by \"left\", \"right\", \"bottom\" or \"top\".\ndefaults::Dict{String,Any}: each zone property's default, by key, for the zones that do not set it.\noptions::Dict{String,Any}: the options set, nested by the parts of their keys: \"solver.method\" is options[\"solver\"][\"method\"]. Only the options ticked in the window are there.\nrun::Dict{String,Any}: the run settings, by key.\nfolder::String: the folder the layout was created in (the window's Create…) or read from; \"\" otherwise.\n\nConstructors\n\nLayout(; L, nel, ni=2, width=nothing, nely=nothing, zones=[], sides=Dict(), defaults=Dict(), options=Dict(),\n         run=Dict(), folder=\"\")\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: when L or nel does not have 2 entries, a size or count is not positive, only one of width and nely is given, or a side is not one of the four.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> l = Layout(; L=[100, 50], nel=[50, 25], zones=[Zone(\"lens\", Ball([50, 20], 5.0); rho=2100)]);\n\njulia> l.L, l.nel, l.ni\n([100.0, 50.0], [50, 25], 2)\n\nSee also save_layout, draw.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.Layout"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.save_layout","page":"Reference","text":"save_layout(file, layout::Layout) -> file\n\nWrite layout to file as TOML, making its folder if needed:\n\n[domain]: L, nel, ni, and width and nely when the layout is extruded;\n[sides], [defaults], [run], and [options] as nested tables;\none [[zone]] table per zone: its name, its shape (shape = \"polygon\" with vertices, or rings for several outlines; \"block\" with lo and hi; \"ball\" with centre and radius) and its properties.\n\nThe folder is not written: it is where the file is.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: when a zone is a Region, a Julia function, which a file cannot hold.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> l = Layout(; L=[10, 5], nel=[10, 5], zones=[Zone(\"base\", Block([0, 0], [10, 2]); rho=2000)]);\n\njulia> file = save_layout(joinpath(mktempdir(), \"model_layout.toml\"), l);\n\njulia> load_layout(file).zones[1].props[:rho]\n2000.0\n\nSee also load_layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.save_layout"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.load_layout","page":"Reference","text":"load_layout(file) -> Layout\n\nRead a layout from its TOML file, in the format save_layout writes. Its folder is the file's folder.\n\nTables a layout does not have, from another program's file (an ElastoPlasm case file, say), are ignored with a warning. A zone without a name is called \"zone i\", by its position.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: when the file has no [domain] table, or a zone's shape is not \"polygon\", \"block\" or \"ball\".\n\nSee also draw, which also takes a layout file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.load_layout"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.draw","page":"Reference","text":"draw(layout::Layout; catalog=Catalog(), step=2, check, save, load, title=\"EasyDraw\", shots=nothing, startdir=pwd())\ndraw(file; kwargs...)\ndraw(; L=[10, 5], nel=[20, 10], ni=2, kwargs...)\n\nOpen the drawing window on a layout, a layout file, or a new domain, and wait until it closes. Returns the Layout when Create… wrote it, nothing after Cancel or when the window is closed.\n\nThe window has four steps, Domain, Zones, Options (only when the catalog has options) and Run; see Drawing in the window. The first window of a session takes about 20 s, while Qt loads; the next ones open at once.\n\nArguments\n\ncatalog: what the window offers: a Catalog, a Dict shaped like its TOML, or a catalog file.\nstep: the step it opens on, 1 (Domain) to 4 (Run): 2 with a layout or a file, 1 for a new domain.\ncheck: check(layout) returns (; errors, warnings, info), vectors of strings shown on the Run step: an error blocks Create…, a warning needs a second click. By default: the number of points, and the zones that reach outside the domain or hold no point.\nsave: save(layout, folder) writes the layout into the folder chosen at Create…; by default as <folder name>_layout.toml (save_layout).\nload: load(file) returns the Layout that \"Start from a file…\" opens; by default load_layout.\ntitle: the window's title.\nshots: (dir, steps): instead of waiting, save a picture of each listed step into dir (step1.png, …) and close. For tests and documentation, offscreen (QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen).\nstartdir: the folder the file and folder dialogs start in.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: when a zone is a Region or a 3D shape, which the window cannot draw.\n\nExamples\n\nl = draw()                                                      # a new domain\nl = draw(\"model_layout.toml\"; catalog=\"catalog.toml\")           # edit a layout, with a host's catalog\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.draw"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EasyDraw.Catalog","page":"Reference","text":"Catalog\n\nWhat the drawing window offers, given by its host program: the properties a zone may set, the boundary kinds, the options and the run settings. An empty catalog gives zones without properties, sides without a kind, and no Options step.\n\nFields\n\nzone::Vector{Field}: the properties a zone may set.\nboundary::Vector{String}: the kinds of boundary a side may be.\ncolors::Vector{String}: the colour each kind is drawn in (\"#4682b4\"), or none.\nside::String: the default kind, the first one unless given.\noptions::Vector{Field}: the options, shown on pages by the first part of their keys.\nrun::Vector{Field}: the run settings.\n\nConstructors\n\nCatalog(; zone=[], boundary=[], colors=[], side=\"\", options=[], run=[])\nCatalog(dict)   # plain data shaped like the catalog's TOML (write_catalog), so a host builds no EasyDraw type\nCatalog(file)   # that TOML file, as read_catalog\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: when there are colours but not one per kind, or side is not one of the kinds.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> c = Catalog(Dict(\"boundary\" => Dict(\"kinds\" => [\"free slip\", \"no slip\"]),\n                        \"zone\"     => [Dict(\"key\" => \"rho\", \"label\" => \"ρ [kg/m³]\", \"default\" => 3300.0)]));\n\njulia> c.side, [f.key for f in c.zone]\n(\"free slip\", [\"rho\"])\n\nSee also write_catalog, draw.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.Catalog"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EasyDraw.Field","page":"Reference","text":"Field\n\nOne entry of a Catalog: a zone property, an option or a run setting, as the window shows it.\n\nFields\n\nkey::String: the key the value is stored under. An option's key starts with its section, the page it is shown on: \"solver.method\".\nkind::Symbol: :menu (one of values), :numeric (any number, or a list of numbers when the default is one; values are presets), :integer (a whole number) or :list (several of values).\nlabel::String: what the window shows; the key by default.\nvalues::Vector{Any}: a menu's or a list's choices; a number's presets.\ndefault::Any: the value when none is set; nothing for none.\nscale::Float64: for a zone property, the factor the window shows its value with (1e-3 shows pascals as kilopascals); the layout keeps the value as given.\nmin::Union{Nothing,Float64}: for an option or a run setting, the smallest number the window accepts.\nshow_if::Dict{String,Vector{Any}}: for a zone property or a run setting, show the field only while each of these options or run settings has one of the listed values. Options are always listed.\nnames::Vector{String}: the names the window shows for values, one per value; none to show the values.\n\nConstructors\n\nField(key, kind=:numeric; label=key, values=[], default=nothing, scale=1, min=nothing, show_if=Dict(), names=[])\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: when kind is not one of the four, or names does not have one name per value.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> f = Field(\"eta\"; label=\"η [Pa s]\", default=1.0e21);\n\njulia> f.kind, f.label\n(:numeric, \"η [Pa s]\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.Field"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.read_catalog","page":"Reference","text":"read_catalog(file) -> Catalog\n\nRead a catalog from its TOML file, in the format write_catalog writes; the same as Catalog(file).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.read_catalog"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.write_catalog","page":"Reference","text":"write_catalog(file, catalog) -> file\n\nWrite catalog (a Catalog, or a Dict shaped like one) to file as TOML, making its folder if needed:\n\n[boundary]: kinds, colors and default;\none [[zone]], [[options]] or [[run]] table per field: key, kind, label, values, default, scale, min, show_if, names, all but key optional when written by hand.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> c = Catalog(; boundary=[\"free slip\", \"no slip\"], zone=[Field(\"rho\"; default=3300.0)]);\n\njulia> file = write_catalog(joinpath(mktempdir(), \"catalog.toml\"), c);\n\njulia> read_catalog(file).zone[1].default\n3300.0\n\nSee also read_catalog.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.write_catalog"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.field_value","page":"Reference","text":"field_value(field::Field, x) -> value\n\nThe value of field for x, a value or the text the window got:\n\nfor a menu, one of its values;\nfor a number, any number (or a list of numbers, when its default is one), at least min, whole for :integer;\nfor a list, a list of its values (or of their names).\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: when x is none of these.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> EasyDraw.field_value(Field(\"nt\", :integer; min=1), \"250\")\n250\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.field_value"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.zone_at","page":"Reference","text":"zone_at(layout, x) -> Int\nzone_at(zones, x) -> Int\n\nThe zone of the point x: the index of the last zone that contains it, or 0 when none does. x is (x, z), or (x, y, z) for an extruded layout.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> l = Layout(; L=[10, 5], nel=[10, 5], zones=[Zone(\"base\", Block([0, 0], [10, 2]); rho=2700),\n                                                   Zone(\"lens\", Ball([5, 2], 1.0); rho=2100)]);\n\njulia> zone_at(l, (5.0, 1.5)), zone_at(l, (1.0, 1.0)), zone_at(l, (1.0, 4.0))\n(2, 1, 0)\n\nSee also zone_index, property.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.zone_at"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.zone_index","page":"Reference","text":"zone_index(layout, xs, zs) -> Matrix{Int}\n\nThe zone of every point (xs[i], zs[j]) of a grid, as zone_at gives it: 0 where no zone holds the point. Use any coordinates: cell centres, vertices, or a staggered grid's velocity points.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> l = Layout(; L=[10, 5], nel=[10, 5], zones=[Zone(\"base\", Block([0, 0], [10, 2]); rho=2700),\n                                                   Zone(\"lens\", Ball([5, 2], 1.0); rho=2100)]);\n\njulia> zone_index(l, [1.0, 5.0], [0.5, 1.5, 4.0])\n2×3 Matrix{Int64}:\n 1  1  0\n 1  2  0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.zone_index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.property","page":"Reference","text":"property(layout, key, xs, zs; default) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nThe property key at every point (xs[i], zs[j]) of a grid: the value its zone sets, or default where the zone does not set it or no zone holds the point. default is the layout's default for key (layout.defaults), else NaN.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: when a zone sets key to something other than a number.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> l = Layout(; L=[10, 5], nel=[10, 5], zones=[Zone(\"base\", Block([0, 0], [10, 2]); rho=2700),\n                                                   Zone(\"lens\", Ball([5, 2], 1.0); rho=2100)]);\n\njulia> property(l, \"rho\", [1.0, 5.0], [0.5, 2.5, 4.0]; default=1000.0)\n2×3 Matrix{Float64}:\n 2700.0  1000.0  1000.0\n 2700.0  2100.0  1000.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.property"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.points","page":"Reference","text":"points(layout) -> (; x, z, zone)\n\nThe points the zones hold: the centres of the ni×ni sub-cells of each cell (ni = 1: the cell centres) that lie in a zone, with the zone of each. These are the points the window previews, e.g. for markers or material points.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> l = Layout(; L=[10, 5], nel=[10, 5], zones=[Zone(\"base\", Block([0, 0], [10, 2]); rho=2700),\n                                                   Zone(\"lens\", Ball([5, 2], 1.0); rho=2100)]);\n\njulia> p = points(l);\n\njulia> length(p.x), count(==(2), p.zone)\n(86, 12)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.points"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.subcell_centres","page":"Reference","text":"subcell_centres(L, nel, ni) -> Vector{Vector{Float64}}\n\nThe centres of the sub-cells of a domain [0, L], with nel cells per axis and ni sub-cells per cell and axis, as one coordinate vector per axis (any dimension): the points points and the window's preview are made of.\n\nThe range arithmetic is ElastoPlasm's for its material points (cell size L/nel, the last node last(0:h:L)), so a preview and a simulation built from the same layout have the same points, to the bit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.subcell_centres"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EasyDraw.Partition","page":"Reference","text":"Partition\n\nA layout as disjoint regions, as partition makes it for a finite-element mesher. Neighbouring regions share their edges and vertices.\n\nFields\n\nvertices::Vector{Vector{Float64}}: the [x, z] vertices.\nedges::Vector{Tuple{Int,Int}}: each edge once, as a pair of vertex indices.\nleft::Vector{Int}, right::Vector{Int}: the region on each side of each edge, left and right of its direction: a zone's index, 0 for the background, -1 outside the domain.\nside::Vector{String}: for an edge on the domain's boundary, its side (\"left\", \"right\", \"bottom\", \"top\"); \"\" inside.\nregions::Vector{@NamedTuple{zone::Int, outer::Vector{Int}, holes::Vector{Vector{Int}}}}: each part of each zone (or of the background, zone 0): its outer outline, counterclockwise, and its holes, clockwise, as loops of vertex indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.Partition"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.partition","page":"Reference","text":"partition(layout; ngon=32, background=true) -> Partition\n\nThe layout as disjoint regions, as a finite-element mesher needs them:\n\neach zone where it applies (where zones overlap, the later one wins), clipped to the domain;\nwith background, the rest of the domain as zone 0;\nshared edges and vertices between neighbouring regions, so that a mesh of them is conforming.\n\nA circle is an inscribed ngon-gon, for itself and for the zones it meets.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: when a zone is a Region or a 3D shape.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> l = Layout(; L=[10, 5], nel=[10, 5], zones=[Zone(\"base\", Block([0, 0], [10, 2]); rho=2700),\n                                                   Zone(\"lens\", Ball([5, 2], 1.0); rho=2100)]);\n\njulia> p = partition(l);\n\njulia> sort([r.zone for r in p.regions])\n3-element Vector{Int64}:\n 0\n 1\n 2\n\nSee also write_geo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.partition"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EasyDraw.write_geo","page":"Reference","text":"write_geo(file, layout; h, ngon=32, background=true) -> file\n\nWrite the layout's partition as a Gmsh script, x–z as Gmsh's x–y, making its folder if needed:\n\na Point per vertex, with mesh size h (by default the layout's cell size);\na Line per edge, shared by the regions on its two sides;\na Plane Surface per region, its outer outline and its holes as Curve Loops;\na Physical Surface per zone name, and one called \"background\";\na Physical Curve per side of the domain: \"left\", \"right\", \"bottom\", \"top\".\n\nMesh it with gmsh file -2, or with Gmsh.jl.\n\nExamples\n\nwrite_geo(\"model.geo\", layout; h=0.5)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EasyDraw.write_geo"},{"category":"section","location":"window/#Drawing-in-the-window","page":"Drawing in the window","text":"using EasyDraw\nl = draw()                                   # a new domain, on the Domain step\nl = draw(l)                                  # edit a layout, on the Zones step\nl = draw(\"model_layout.toml\")                # edit a layout file\nl = draw(l; catalog=\"catalog.toml\")          # with what your program offers (see Catalogs)\n\ndraw opens the window and waits. Create… writes the layout into a folder you choose and returns it; Cancel, or closing the window, returns nothing. The first window of a session takes about 20 s, while Qt loads; the next ones open at once.\n\nThe window has a canvas on the left and four steps on the right, as tabs: Domain, Zones, Options (only when the catalog has options) and Run. You can go back and forth between them at any time.\n\nThe pictures on this page show a slope over two layers, with a lens, drawn with a small catalog: two zone properties (ρ and η), three boundary kinds, three options and two run settings.","title":"Drawing in the window"},{"category":"section","location":"window/#Domain","page":"Drawing in the window","text":"(Image: The Domain step)\n\nSize: drag the white handle at the domain's upper-right corner, or type Lx, Lz and the cell size h. Cells are square: the numbers of cells follow from h, shown under the fields.\nWidth y, 3D: a width extrudes the drawing along y; leave it blank for 2D.\nPoints per cell: the canvas previews ni×ni points per cell, at the centres of the sub-cells: the points points returns.\nBoundaries: a boundary kind per side, from the catalog. Each side is drawn in its kind's colour.\nStart from a file… opens a layout file (or your program's own file, see Catalogs), replacing what is drawn.\nLoad catalog… replaces the catalog with a catalog file's (see Catalogs).","title":"Domain"},{"category":"section","location":"window/#Picture-to-draw-on","page":"Drawing in the window","text":"Choose Picture to draw on to trace zones over a scanned cross-section or a map, any picture Qt reads:\n\nPick the picture. It opens across the domain's width, under the grid.\nSet its scale: Measure a scale bar, click the bar's two ends on the picture, type its length in metres and press Enter (Esc cancels). Or type the picture's width.\nPlace it: its lower-left corner (x, z, 0 by default; a negative value crops it) and its opacity.\n\nThe domain follows the picture, from the origin to the picture's far corner, in whole cells of size h; typing Lx or Lz afterwards still changes it. Choosing Domain again hides the picture and keeps it. The picture is a drawing aid only: it is not saved with the layout.","title":"Picture to draw on"},{"category":"section","location":"window/#Zones","page":"Drawing in the window","text":"(Image: The Zones step)\n\n+ New zone ▾ draws a new zone:\n\nDraw: a polygon. Click its vertices, then click the first vertex again, or press Enter, to close it.\nRectangle: two opposite corners.\nTriangle: three corners; it closes on the third.\nCircle: its centre, then a point on it.\n\nA new zone is cut out of the zones it overlaps: their outlines then follow it and share its vertices. Zones written in code may still overlap; there, the later zone wins, and the earlier one is drawn dashed under it.\n\nSnapping. Clicks snap to existing vertices first, then to edges (snap shapes), then to the sub-cells' corners, every h/ni (snap grid), so a vertex never sits on a point. The cursor shows what it snapped to: a square on a vertex, a diamond on an edge, a ring on the grid. A vertex placed on another zone's edge is inserted into that edge, so the two zones share it.\n\nEditing:\n\nDrag a vertex to move it, in every zone that shares it. Its coordinates can then be typed exactly in the vertex row; × deletes it.\nClick an edge to insert a vertex there; right-click a vertex to delete it.\nDrag a circle's centre or its handle, or type its centre and radius in the circle row.\nClick inside a zone to select it, or pick it in the list.\n\nThe selected zone: its name and its properties, from the catalog. A blank property takes the default shown beside it. ↑ and ↓ move the zone in the list: a zone lower in the list wins where zones overlap. delete removes it; undo and redo undo and redo any edit. points shows or hides the points; the line under the help text counts them per zone.\n\nKeys:\n\nKey While drawing a zone Otherwise\nEnter close the polygon \nBackspace remove the last vertex \nEsc cancel the zone \nCmd/Ctrl + Z remove the last vertex undo\nShift + Cmd/Ctrl + Z, or Cmd/Ctrl + Y  redo","title":"Zones"},{"category":"section","location":"window/#Moving-around","page":"Drawing in the window","text":"These work on every step:\n\nZoom: the mouse wheel, a trackpad pinch, or Cmd/Ctrl + scroll, about the pointer.\nPan: drag the empty canvas, drag with the middle button (also while drawing), or move two fingers on a trackpad.\nFit view (upper-right corner of the canvas) recentres on the domain.","title":"Moving around"},{"category":"section","location":"window/#Options","page":"Drawing in the window","text":"(Image: The Options step)\n\nThe catalog's options, on pages by the first part of their keys (solver.method is on page solver), picked in the drop-down at the top. An option is used only when its box is ticked: picking or typing a value ticks it, and unticking it restores the default. A number can be typed, or picked from its presets; a list option is a grid of check boxes.\n\nThe ticked options are in the layout's options, nested by the parts of their keys:\n\nl.options[\"solver\"][\"method\"]                               # set only when ticked\nget(get(l.options, \"solver\", Dict()), \"method\", \"direct\")   # with the default","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"window/#Run","page":"Drawing in the window","text":"(Image: The Run step)\n\nThe catalog's run settings, then the Checks: what your program reports on the layout, by default the number of points and the zones that reach outside the domain or hold no point. An error blocks Create…; a warning needs a second click on it.\n\nCreate… asks for a folder (choose one, or make one with the dialog's New Folder button) and writes the layout there, by default as <folder name>_layout.toml. The window closes and draw returns the layout, whose folder is that folder. Cancel closes the window and returns nothing.\n\nThe run settings are in the layout's run:\n\nl.run[\"nt\"]","title":"Run"},{"category":"section","location":"catalog/#Catalogs","page":"Catalogs","text":"The window knows nothing of your program. What it offers comes from a Catalog that your program passes to draw:\n\nthe properties a zone may set (ρ, η, …), on the Zones step;\nthe boundary kinds a side may be, on the Domain step;\nthe options, on the Options step, which is shown only when there are some;\nthe run settings, on the Run step.\n\nWithout a catalog, zones have no properties and sides no kind: the window still draws zones.","title":"Catalogs"},{"category":"section","location":"catalog/#As-a-file","page":"Catalogs","text":"A catalog is easiest written as TOML, and passed as the file itself:\n\n[boundary]\nkinds   = [\"free slip\", \"no slip\"]\ncolors  = [\"#4682b4\", \"#b22222\"]\ndefault = \"free slip\"\n\n[[zone]]\nkey = \"rho\"\nlabel = \"ρ [kg/m³]\"\ndefault = 3300.0\n\n[[zone]]\nkey = \"eta\"\nlabel = \"η [Pa s]\"\ndefault = 1.0e21\n\n[[options]]                       # on the page \"solver\", the first part of the key\nkey = \"solver.method\"\nkind = \"menu\"\nvalues = [\"direct\", \"iterative\"]\ndefault = \"direct\"\n\n[[run]]\nkey = \"nt\"\nkind = \"integer\"\nlabel = \"time steps\"\ndefault = 1000\nmin = 1\n\nl = draw(; catalog=\"catalog.toml\")\n\nThe window's Load catalog… (Domain step) loads another catalog file without restarting it; see Changing the catalog below.","title":"As a file"},{"category":"section","location":"catalog/#Fields","page":"Catalogs","text":"Each [[zone]], [[options]] and [[run]] table is a Field. Its key is required; the rest is optional.\n\nkind the value the window shows\n\"numeric\" any number; a list of numbers when the default is one; values are presets a field, and presets\n\"integer\" a whole number a field\n\"menu\" one of values a drop-down\n\"list\" several of values check boxes\n\n\"numeric\" is the default kind. A field can also set:\n\nlabel: what the window shows, the key by default;\ndefault: the value when none is set;\nnames: the names the window shows for values, one per value;\nscale, for a zone property: the factor its value is shown with, e.g. 0.001 to show pascals as kilopascals. The layout keeps the value as given, in pascals;\nmin, for an option or a run setting: the smallest number accepted;\nshow_if, for a zone property or a run setting: show it only while each listed option or run setting has one of the listed values, e.g. show_if = { \"solver.method\" = [\"iterative\"] }. Options are always listed.\n\nAn option's key starts with its section, the page it is shown on: \"solver.method\" is on page solver.","title":"Fields"},{"category":"section","location":"catalog/#In-code","page":"Catalogs","text":"A catalog can also be built from Fields, or given as plain data, a Dict shaped like the TOML. The latter lets a program describe its catalog without depending on EasyDraw's types:\n\ncatalog = Catalog(; boundary=[\"free slip\", \"no slip\"],\n                    zone=[Field(\"rho\"; label=\"ρ [kg/m³]\", default=3300.0)],\n                    run=[Field(\"nt\", :integer; label=\"time steps\", default=1000, min=1)])\ncatalog = Dict(\"boundary\" => Dict(\"kinds\" => [\"free slip\", \"no slip\"]),\n               \"zone\"     => [Dict(\"key\" => \"rho\", \"label\" => \"ρ [kg/m³]\", \"default\" => 3300.0)])\nwrite_catalog(\"catalog.toml\", catalog)      # either, as the file","title":"In code"},{"category":"section","location":"catalog/#What-comes-back","page":"Catalogs","text":"The layout draw returns holds what was set:\n\neach zone's properties, in zone.props (by symbol, z.props[:rho]); a blank property is not set, and takes the layout's defaults;\nthe boundary kind of each side, in l.sides;\nthe options, in l.options, nested by the parts of their keys, and only those ticked: get(get(l.options, \"solver\", Dict()), \"method\", \"direct\");\nthe run settings, in l.run: l.run[\"nt\"].","title":"What comes back"},{"category":"section","location":"catalog/#Your-program's-own-hooks","page":"Catalogs","text":"What a file cannot hold, your program passes as functions:\n\nl = draw(layout; catalog,\n         check = l -> (; errors=String[], warnings=String[], info=[\"$(length(points(l).x)) markers\"]),\n         save  = (l, folder) -> my_write(joinpath(folder, \"model.toml\"), l),\n         load  = file -> my_read(file),\n         title = \"MySolver\")\n\ncheck(layout): the checks on the Run step, as (; errors, warnings, info): an error blocks Create…, a warning needs a second click.\nsave(layout, folder): what Create… writes into the chosen folder.\nload(file): what Start from a file… reads, returning a Layout.\ntitle: the window's title.\n\nElastoPlasm.jl's draw_case is such a program; see With ElastoPlasm.jl.","title":"Your program's own hooks"},{"category":"section","location":"catalog/#Changing-the-catalog","page":"Catalogs","text":"Load catalog… replaces the catalog with another file's, e.g. your solver's, while drawing:\n\nNothing drawn is lost. Each zone keeps all its properties: those the new catalog lists are shown, the others are carried through.\nOption overrides and run settings that are still valid are kept; the old catalog's other ones are dropped.\nA side whose boundary kind the new catalog lacks takes the new default.\nUndo does not undo the load: load the previous catalog back instead.","title":"Changing the catalog"},{"category":"section","location":"code/#Layouts-in-code","page":"Layouts in code","text":"A Layout is the whole model: a domain, its zones, a boundary kind per side, and the values your program asks for in the window. What the window draws, code can write, and the other way round:\n\njulia> using EasyDraw\n\njulia> l = Layout(; L=[20, 10], nel=[20, 10],                  # [0, 20] × [0, 10] m, 20 × 10 cells\n                    zones=[Zone(\"base\",  Block([0, 0], [20, 4]); rho=2700),\n                           Zone(\"wedge\", Polygon([[0, 4], [20, 4], [0, 8]]); rho=1900),\n                           Zone(\"lens\",  Ball([14, 3], 1.5); rho=2100)],\n                    sides=Dict(\"bottom\" => \"no slip\"), defaults=Dict(\"rho\" => 2000.0));\n\njulia> [z.name for z in l.zones]\n3-element Vector{String}:\n \"base\"\n \"wedge\"\n \"lens\"","title":"Layouts in code"},{"category":"section","location":"code/#Shapes","page":"Layouts in code","text":"Shape The points it holds\nPolygon(vertices) inside the outline, [x, z] vertices (even–odd rule, so concave works)\nPolygon(rings) inside an odd number of the outlines: several parts, or holes\nBlock(lo, hi) the axis-aligned box, its bounds included\nBall(centre, radius) the disk, its circle included\nRegion(x -> …) where the function is true: any shape, in code only\n\ninside(shape, x) tells whether a point lies in a shape. A Region cannot be written to a file, drawn in the window or partitioned; the others can. In the window, a block becomes its rectangle, a polygon with the same points.","title":"Shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"code/#Zones","page":"Layouts in code","text":"A Zone is a name, a shape and properties of any name: Zone(\"lens\", Ball([14, 3], 1.5); rho=2100). A number is stored as a Float64, a list of numbers as a Vector{Float64}, anything else as given.\n\nWhere zones overlap, the later one wins: a point belongs to the last zone that contains it. Above, the lens lies partly in the base and wins there:\n\njulia> zone_at(l, (14.0, 3.0))                                 # in the lens, and in the base below it\n3\n\nSo a model can be written from the bottom up, each zone over the ones before it. In the window, a new zone is cut out of the earlier ones instead, so drawn zones do not overlap.","title":"Zones"},{"category":"section","location":"code/#The-rest-of-a-layout","page":"Layouts in code","text":"ni: points per cell and axis, 2 by default; see points.\nwidth and nely: a width and a number of cells along y, to extrude the model in 3D.\nsides: a boundary kind per side, \"left\", \"right\", \"bottom\" or \"top\"; the kinds are your program's (see Catalogs).\ndefaults: each zone property's default, for the zones that do not set it.\noptions and run: your program's options and run settings, as the window sets them (see Catalogs).\n\nValues are kept as given: SI in, SI out.","title":"The rest of a layout"},{"category":"section","location":"code/#Files","page":"Layouts in code","text":"save_layout writes a layout as TOML, load_layout reads it, and draw opens one:\n\nsave_layout(\"model_layout.toml\", l)\nl = load_layout(\"model_layout.toml\")\nl = draw(\"model_layout.toml\")\n\nThe file is meant to be read and edited by hand too. The layout above, with an option and a run setting:\n\n[defaults]\nrho = 2000.0\n\n[domain]\nL = [20.0, 10.0]\nnel = [20, 10]\nni = 2\n\n[options.solver]\nmethod = \"iterative\"\n\n[run]\nnt = 500\n\n[sides]\nbottom = \"no slip\"\n\n[[zone]]\nhi = [20.0, 4.0]\nlo = [0.0, 0.0]\nname = \"base\"\nrho = 2700.0\nshape = \"block\"\n[[zone]]\nname = \"wedge\"\nrho = 1900.0\nshape = \"polygon\"\nvertices = [[0.0, 4.0], [20.0, 4.0], [0.0, 8.0]]\n[[zone]]\ncentre = [14.0, 3.0]\nname = \"lens\"\nradius = 1.5\nrho = 2100.0\nshape = \"ball\"\n\nA polygon of several outlines is written as rings = [[…], […]] instead of vertices. The zones come in order: the later one wins where they overlap. A layout's folder is not written: it is the folder the file is in.","title":"Files"},{"category":"section","location":"grids/#Finite-differences-and-particles","page":"Finite differences and particles","text":"A grid-based code needs the model at its own points: the zone of each, or a property's field. EasyDraw evaluates a layout at any coordinates: cell centres, vertices, or a staggered grid's velocity points.\n\njulia> using EasyDraw\n\njulia> l = Layout(; L=[100, 50], nel=[50, 25],\n                    zones=[Zone(\"mantle\", Block([0, 0], [100, 40]); rho=3300.0, eta=1e21),\n                           Zone(\"plume\",  Ball([50, 15], 8.0);       rho=3200.0, eta=1e19)]);\n\njulia> xc, zc = 1.0:2.0:99.0, 1.0:2.0:49.0;                    # the cell centres\n\njulia> phase = zone_index(l, xc, zc);                          # the zone of each, 0 where none\n\njulia> size(phase), phase[25, 8], phase[1, 1], phase[1, 25]\n((50, 25), 2, 1, 0)","title":"Finite differences and particles"},{"category":"section","location":"grids/#A-property's-field","page":"Finite differences and particles","text":"property gives a property at every point: the value of the point's zone, or a default where the zone does not set it or no zone holds the point.\n\njulia> eta = property(l, \"eta\", xc, zc; default=1e18);         # 1e18 above the mantle\n\njulia> eta[25, 8], eta[1, 1], eta[1, 25]\n(1.0e19, 1.0e21, 1.0e18)\n\nWithout default, it is the layout's default for that property (l.defaults), else NaN.","title":"A property's field"},{"category":"section","location":"grids/#One-point","page":"Finite differences and particles","text":"zone_at gives the zone of one point: the index of the last zone that contains it, 0 for none.\n\njulia> zone_at(l, (50.0, 15.0)), l.zones[2].name\n(2, \"plume\")\n\nFor an extruded layout (a width and nely), points are (x, y, z).","title":"One point"},{"category":"section","location":"grids/#Markers-and-material-points","page":"Finite differences and particles","text":"points gives points spread evenly in the zones, ni×ni per cell at the centres of the sub-cells, with the zone of each: markers for a marker-in-cell code, or material points. They are the points the window previews.\n\njulia> p = points(l);                                          # ni = 2: 4 per cell\n\njulia> length(p.x) == length(p.z) == length(p.zone)\ntrue","title":"Markers and material points"},{"category":"section","location":"#EasyDraw.jl","page":"Home","text":"EasyDraw lets you draw the zones of a 2D (x–z) model, by hand on a domain or over a scanned cross-section, and get them back as data for your solver:\n\nFinite differences, particles: the zone or a property at any coordinates, e.g. on your grid.\nFinite elements: disjoint regions with shared edges, written as a Gmsh script.\n\nThe same zones can be written in code instead of drawn. A point belongs to the last zone that contains it, so a zone drawn later overrides the ones below it.\n\n(Image: The Zones step of the window: a slope over two layers, with a lens)","title":"EasyDraw.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Install","page":"Home","text":"EasyDraw is not registered yet:\n\n] add https://github.com/ewyser/EasyDraw.jl\n\nThis installs the window too (Qt Quick, through QML.jl). using EasyDraw itself is instant: Qt loads with the first window of a session, which takes about 20 s (the next ones open at once). Julia 1.10 or later.","title":"Install"},{"category":"section","location":"#A-first-model","page":"Home","text":"Draw it:\n\nusing EasyDraw\nl = draw()                          # a new domain; returns a Layout, or nothing if cancelled\n\nor write it:\n\nl = Layout(; L=[100.0, 50.0], nel=[50, 25],                     # [0, 100] × [0, 50] m, 50 × 25 cells\n             zones=[Zone(\"mantle\", Block([0, 0], [100, 40]); rho=3300.0, eta=1e21),\n                    Zone(\"plume\",  Ball([50, 15], 8.0);       rho=3200.0, eta=1e19)])\nsave_layout(\"plume_layout.toml\", l)\nl = draw(\"plume_layout.toml\")       # and edit it in the window\n\nthen use it:\n\nrho = property(l, \"rho\", 1.0:2.0:99.0, 1.0:2.0:49.0)   # on your grid's cell centres\nwrite_geo(\"plume.geo\", l)                              # or as regions for Gmsh","title":"A first model"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation","page":"Home","text":"The documentation has a guide to the window, step by step, and pages on layouts in code, sampling on a grid, meshing with Gmsh, catalogs (what your program offers in the window) and ElastoPlasm.jl, then the reference of every function.","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Limits","page":"Home","text":"2D (x–z) only. A width and nely extrude a layout along y, and zone_at takes (x, y, z) points.\nThe picture is not saved. It is a drawing aid only, not stored with the layout.\nZones are cut only when one is drawn. Moving a zone afterwards can leave an overlap (the later zone wins) or a gap.","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"#Tests","page":"Home","text":"] test EasyDraw\n\nThey cover the shapes, layouts and catalogs, sampling, the partition and its .geo, the editor driven as a user would (drawing, snapping, cutting, undo, the picture, options, run, Create…), and the window itself, offscreen.\n\nMIT license.","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"elastoplasm/#With-ElastoPlasm.jl","page":"With ElastoPlasm.jl","text":"ElastoPlasm.jl, a material point method solver, uses EasyDraw for its shapes, its zones and its drawing window. The two share the points, so a model moves between them without loss:\n\nusing ElastoPlasm\ncase = draw_case()                           # the window, with ElastoPlasm's catalog; returns a case\ncase = slump_case([64.16, 16.04], [40, 10])\nsave_layout(\"slump_layout.toml\", case)       # a layout file, for EasyDraw alone (values in SI)\nwrite_geo(\"slump.geo\", case)                 # a case works wherever EasyDraw takes a layout:\np = points(case)                             #   its points are the simulation's material points, to the bit\ncase = load_case(\"drawn_layout.toml\")        # and back: a layout drawn with ElastoPlasm's catalog is a case\n\nA layout drawn with EasyDraw alone becomes a case when it was drawn with ElastoPlasm's catalog: write it with write_catalog(\"elastoplasm_catalog.toml\", get_catalog()), then load that file with Load catalog….\n\nOpened in EasyDraw directly, an ElastoPlasm case file (*_case.toml) loses its material, boundary conditions and solver options, and its friction angles stay in degrees: load_layout warns. Convert it first with save_layout(file, load_case(case_file)).","title":"With ElastoPlasm.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"meshes/#Finite-elements-(Gmsh)","page":"Finite elements (Gmsh)","text":"A finite-element mesher needs the model as disjoint regions that share their edges, so that the mesh is conforming across them. write_geo writes them as a Gmsh script; partition gives the same as data.\n\nusing EasyDraw\nl = load_layout(\"model_layout.toml\")\nwrite_geo(\"model.geo\", l; h=1.0)          # mesh size h, by default the layout's cell size\n\nThen mesh it with Gmsh, gmsh model.geo -2, or from Julia with Gmsh.jl.","title":"Finite elements (Gmsh)"},{"category":"section","location":"meshes/#The-regions","page":"Finite elements (Gmsh)","text":"Each zone where it applies: where zones overlap, the later one wins. A zone reaching outside the domain is clipped to it.\nThe rest of the domain as the background, zone 0 (background=false leaves it out).\nNeighbouring regions share their edges and vertices exactly.\nA circle is an inscribed polygon of ngon sides (32 by default), for itself and for the zones it meets.\n\nRegion zones cannot be partitioned: they have no outline.","title":"The regions"},{"category":"section","location":"meshes/#The-script","page":"Finite elements (Gmsh)","text":"For a 4 × 2 m domain whose lower half is a zone called \"base\":\n\nwrite_geo(\"model.geo\", Layout(; L=[4, 2], nel=[4, 2], zones=[Zone(\"base\", Block([0, 0], [4, 1]))]))\n\n// EasyDraw: a layout's partition, x–z as x–y. Mesh it with: gmsh model.geo -2\nh = 1.0;\nPoint(1) = {0.0, 0.0, 0, h};\nPoint(2) = {4.0, 0.0, 0, h};\nPoint(3) = {4.0, 2.0, 0, h};\nPoint(4) = {0.0, 2.0, 0, h};\nPoint(5) = {4.0, 1.0, 0, h};\nPoint(6) = {0.0, 1.0, 0, h};\nLine(1) = {1, 2};\nLine(2) = {1, 6};\nLine(3) = {2, 5};\nLine(4) = {3, 4};\nLine(5) = {3, 5};\nLine(6) = {4, 6};\nLine(7) = {5, 6};\nCurve Loop(1) = {4, 6, -7, -5};\nPlane Surface(1) = {1};\nCurve Loop(2) = {1, 3, 7, -2};\nPlane Surface(2) = {2};\nPhysical Surface(\"background\") = {1};\nPhysical Surface(\"base\") = {2};\nPhysical Curve(\"left\") = {2, 6};\nPhysical Curve(\"right\") = {3, 5};\nPhysical Curve(\"bottom\") = {1};\nPhysical Curve(\"top\") = {4};\n\nThe x–z plane is Gmsh's x–y plane. Line 7 is the interface between the two regions: both surfaces use it. Each zone name is a Physical Surface (all its parts, if it has several), and each side of the domain a Physical Curve, to set material properties and boundary conditions on in your code.","title":"The script"},{"category":"section","location":"meshes/#As-data","page":"Finite elements (Gmsh)","text":"partition returns a Partition: the vertices, each edge once with the region on its left and on its right (a zone's index, 0 for the background, -1 outside the domain) and the domain side it lies on, and each region's outer outline (counterclockwise) and holes (clockwise), as loops of vertex indices.\n\njulia> using EasyDraw\n\njulia> p = partition(Layout(; L=[4, 2], nel=[4, 2], zones=[Zone(\"base\", Block([0, 0], [4, 1]))]));\n\njulia> [(r.zone, r.outer) for r in p.regions]\n2-element Vector{Tuple{Int64, Vector{Int64}}}:\n (0, [3, 4, 6, 5])\n (1, [1, 2, 5, 6])","title":"As data"}]
}
